/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · life: work and career in Australia */
WB.L("resume-that-gets-read", "life", "beginner", "Writing a resume that gets read", "A recruiter might give your resume less than a minute on the first pass. Here is how to make those seconds count, step by step.",
  [
    ["The first skim", "Your resume is first skimmed, not read, so make the top third do the heavy lifting.", "Picture a hiring manager with sixty applications, a cold coffee and a meeting in twenty minutes. They are not reading every line. They glance at the top of page one, scan your most recent job title, look for a few words that match the ad and decide whether to keep going. That means your resume has two audiences: the busy human skimming it and, at many larger employers, software that sorts applications before a person sees them. The good news is that both reward the same things: a clear layout, plain headings, relevant words near the top and evidence that you can do the job. Before you write anything, print the job ad and highlight every skill, tool and duty it mentions. That highlighted list is your brief."],
    ["Structure that works in Australia", "Use a simple, predictable order: contact details, summary, experience, skills, education.", "Australian resumes tend to follow a familiar pattern, and familiar is good because readers know where to look. Start with your name, phone, email, suburb and state, and a LinkedIn link if you keep it current. You do not need your full street address, a photo, your date of birth or your marital status, and leaving them out also helps protect you from unconscious bias. Next comes a short summary of two or three sentences, then your work history in reverse order, most recent first. After that, list key skills, then education and any licences or tickets, such as a white card, RSA or driver's licence. Two to three pages is common for experienced workers; one or two is fine early in your career. Use a clean font, generous margins and consistent dates."],
    ["Write achievements, not duties", "Show what changed because you were there, using numbers where you honestly can.", "Most resumes read like job descriptions: 'responsible for customer service', 'duties included stock control'. That tells the reader what the role was, not how well you did it. Swap duties for achievements. A useful pattern is action, task and result. Instead of 'handled customer complaints', try 'resolved escalated complaints for a busy retail store, helping lift our customer survey score over six months'. Numbers make claims concrete: how many customers, how big a budget, how much time saved, how many people trained. Only use figures you can explain in an interview, because good interviewers will ask. If you cannot measure something, describe the scale or the outcome instead. Start each line with a strong verb such as led, built, reduced, organised, trained or launched, and cut filler words wherever you find them."],
    ["Tailor it every time", "A tailored resume beats a generic one, and tailoring takes less time than you think.", "Sending the same resume to fifty jobs feels efficient but usually is not. Keep a master document with everything you have ever done, then make a copy for each application. Go back to your highlighted job ad and make sure the most relevant experience appears near the top and uses the same language. If the ad says 'stakeholder engagement' and you wrote 'working with other teams', use their phrase, as long as it is honest. Reorder your skills so the ones they asked for come first. Trim older or unrelated roles to a single line. Many employers use applicant tracking systems that search for keywords, so matching terms can matter, but stuffing in words you cannot back up will backfire with the human who reads it next. Twenty focused minutes per application is a sensible target."],
    ["The summary and the cover letter", "A sharp summary and a short, specific cover letter tell the reader why you, why this job.", "Your summary sits at the top and answers one question: who are you, professionally, and what do you offer? Something like 'Hospitality supervisor with six years in busy venues, strong at rostering, training new staff and keeping service calm on big nights' works far better than 'hard-working team player seeking new challenges'. If the ad asks for a cover letter, keep it to about a page. Open by naming the role and one genuine reason you want it. In the middle, pick two or three requirements from the ad and give a brief example of how you meet each. Close by saying you would welcome the chance to talk. Address it to a real person if the ad names one. Never send a letter with another company's name left in, a classic and costly mistake."],
    ["Common traps to avoid", "Small errors and vague claims sink more applications than missing experience does.", "Recruiters often say the fastest way to the 'no' pile is a typo in the first few lines. Read your resume aloud, slowly, then ask someone else to read it too. Watch for inconsistent dates, mixed tenses and an unprofessional email address. Avoid long blocks of text; short lines with clear headings are easier to scan. Leave out reasons for leaving each job, salary history and personal details that do not relate to the work. Do not exaggerate titles or qualifications: employers can and do check, and a stretched claim can cost you the job later. Gaps in your work history are normal and do not need long explanations; a brief line such as 'career break, caring for family' or 'travel and study' is enough. Save and send the file as a PDF unless the employer asks otherwise."],
    ["Referees and final checks", "Line up referees before you apply and give them a heads up about each role.", "Most Australian employers check references before making an offer, usually by phone. Choose two or three people who have supervised your work recently, such as a manager or team leader, rather than friends or family. Always ask permission first, confirm their current phone number and email, and tell them which jobs you are going for so they can speak to what matters. Many people write 'referees available on request', which is perfectly acceptable and keeps their details private until needed. Before you hit send, run one final checklist: the file name includes your name, the contact details are correct, the job title in your cover letter matches the ad and the most relevant achievements sit on page one. Then keep a simple spreadsheet of where you applied, when and what happened, so you can follow up."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Which of these is generally best left off an Australian resume?", ["Your suburb and state", "Your date of birth", "Your LinkedIn link", "Your licences and tickets"], 1, "Personal details such as date of birth are not needed and leaving them out helps reduce bias."],
    [2, "What turns a duty into an achievement?", ["Listing more duties", "Adding a photo", "Showing the result you produced", "Using longer sentences"], 2, "Achievements show what changed because of your work, ideally with honest numbers."],
    [3, "Why mirror the job ad's language when it is honest to do so?", ["It helps both keyword software and skimming humans spot the match", "It is a legal requirement", "It makes the resume longer", "Recruiters only read the last page"], 0, "Many employers use tracking systems that search for keywords, and humans skim for the same terms."],
    [5, "How should you handle a gap in your work history?", ["Hide it by changing dates", "Write a full page explaining it", "Leave the whole job history off", "Note it briefly and honestly"], 3, "Gaps are common, and a short honest line is enough."],
    [6, "What should you do before listing someone as a referee?", ["Nothing, employers rarely call", "Ask permission and tell them about the role", "Choose a close friend", "Give their details only to the recruiter's competitor"], 1, "Referees should agree in advance and know what job you are going for."]
  ]);

WB.L("reading-your-payslip", "life", "beginner", "Reading your payslip", "That little document on payday tells you whether you are being paid correctly. Most people never really read it. Here is how.",
  [
    ["Why your payslip matters", "Your payslip is your best everyday check that you are being paid what you are owed.", "Imagine working the same Saturday shifts for a year, only to discover your weekend penalty rate was never applied. Underpayment in Australia is more common than many people realise, and it is not always deliberate: payroll errors, wrong award classifications and outdated rates all happen. Your payslip is where these mistakes show up first. In Australia, employers generally must give employees a payslip within one working day of payday, either on paper or electronically, and it must include certain details. Reading it each pay takes two minutes and can save you hundreds or even thousands of dollars over time. This lesson is general information, not legal or financial advice, and the Fair Work Ombudsman at fairwork.gov.au is the official place to check your specific situation."],
    ["What must be on it", "The law sets out the information a payslip has to show, so you know what to look for.", "A compliant payslip should name your employer, including their ABN, and name you. It should show the pay period and the date you were paid. It must show your gross pay, which is the total before tax, and your net pay, which is what lands in your bank account. It should list any deductions, such as tax or salary sacrifice, and any bonuses, loadings, allowances or penalty rates. If you are paid by the hour, it should show your hourly rate and how many hours were paid at that rate. It should also show the superannuation your employer is paying for you and the fund it is going to. If any of these are missing, it is worth politely asking your payroll team why."],
    ["Gross, net and tax", "The gap between gross and net pay is mostly tax withheld and sent to the ATO for you.", "Under the pay as you go system, your employer withholds tax from each pay and sends it to the Australian Taxation Office. How much they withhold depends on how much you earn, how often you are paid and what you told them on your tax file number declaration when you started. One key choice on that form is whether to claim the tax-free threshold. Most people claim it from one employer only, usually their main job. If you claim it from two jobs at once, too little tax may be withheld and you could face a bill at tax time. The ATO website has calculators that estimate how much tax should come out of a given pay, which is a handy cross check if something looks off."],
    ["Super on your payslip", "Check that super is being paid, at the right rate, into the fund you chose.", "Superannuation is money your employer pays on top of your wages into a retirement fund. The super guarantee is a set percentage of your ordinary time earnings, and the rate has risen in steps over the years, so check the current figure on the ATO website. Your payslip should show how much super has been calculated for the period. But a payslip only shows what your employer says it will pay, not what actually arrived. Every few months, log in to your super fund's app or website, or check through myGov, and confirm the contributions are landing. Unpaid super is a common problem and can be hard to spot without checking. If money is missing, raise it with your employer first, and if that does not fix it, the ATO has a process for reporting unpaid super."],
    ["Rates, penalties and awards", "Your base rate and any penalty rates usually come from an award or agreement, and you can look them up.", "Many Australian workers are covered by a modern award, a legal document that sets minimum pay and conditions for an industry or occupation. Others are covered by an enterprise agreement negotiated at their workplace. These set your minimum base rate and extras such as casual loading, overtime and penalty rates for evenings, weekends and public holidays. To check yours, use the Fair Work Ombudsman's pay calculator or download the pay guide for your award. You will need to know your award, your classification level and whether you are full time, part time or casual. Minimum rates are usually reviewed each year, with changes commonly starting from the first full pay period on or after 1 July, so a rate that was right last year might be wrong now."],
    ["A quick payslip routine", "Spend two minutes each payday comparing your hours, rate and super against what you expect.", "Build a simple habit. Keep a note of your hours each week, in your phone or a diary, including start and finish times and breaks. When your payslip arrives, compare the hours paid with your record. Check that each type of hour, such as ordinary time, overtime or Sunday work, is paid at the right rate. Glance at the year to date totals, which add up your earnings, tax and super since 1 July and should grow steadily. Make sure your leave balances look sensible if you are permanent, and check that any leave you took was paid. Keep every payslip, ideally saved as a PDF in one folder, because records are your best evidence if anything ever needs to be fixed. Old payslips can also help at tax time."],
    ["If something looks wrong", "Raise errors calmly and in writing first, then know where to get help.", "Most pay problems are fixed with a polite conversation. Start by checking your own numbers, then write a short message to your manager or payroll: say what you noticed, what you believe the correct amount is and how you worked it out, and ask them to look into it. Keep a copy. Give them a reasonable chance to respond. If the problem is not fixed, the Fair Work Ombudsman offers free information, templates for raising issues and, in some cases, help resolving disputes. For unpaid super, contact the ATO. If you are a union member, your union can also help. Be aware that there are time limits for recovering unpaid wages, so do not let a problem sit for years. Raising a genuine pay concern is a workplace right, and employers must not punish you for it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How soon after payday must an employer generally provide a payslip?", ["Within one working day", "Within one month", "Only at the end of the financial year", "Only if you ask"], 0, "Employers generally must provide a payslip within one working day of payday."],
    [2, "What is the main reason net pay is lower than gross pay?", ["Super is taken out of your wages", "Tax is withheld and sent to the ATO", "The bank keeps a fee", "Leave is deducted every week"], 1, "Under PAYG, employers withhold tax from each pay and send it to the ATO."],
    [3, "Why check your super fund as well as your payslip?", ["Payslips never show super", "Super is optional for most employers", "The payslip shows what is owed, not proof it arrived", "Your fund sets your wage"], 2, "A payslip shows what the employer says it will pay, so confirm contributions are actually landing."],
    [4, "Which official tool helps you check minimum award rates?", ["The ASX website", "Your bank's budgeting app", "The Australian Bureau of Statistics census", "The Fair Work Ombudsman's pay calculator"], 3, "The FWO pay calculator and pay guides show minimum rates for each award."],
    [6, "What is a good first step if your pay seems wrong?", ["Quit immediately", "Post about it on social media", "Raise it calmly in writing with payroll or your manager", "Ignore it until tax time"], 2, "Most errors are fixed with a clear written query, and help is available if that fails."]
  ]);

WB.L("asking-for-a-pay-rise", "life", "intermediate", "Asking for a pay rise", "Many people wait years to be offered more money. Here is how to prepare for, start and handle the pay rise conversation yourself.",
  [
    ["Why people do not ask", "Silence rarely gets you paid more, and most managers expect the question at some point.", "Sam has been in the same role for three years. She has trained two new starters, taken on the rostering and quietly fixed the ordering system. Her pay has only moved when the award minimum went up. Sam is not alone: plenty of people never ask, because it feels awkward, greedy or risky. Yet from a manager's point of view, a well prepared request from a valued employee is a normal part of running a team, and far better than that person resigning without warning. Asking does not guarantee a yes. But a clear, evidence based conversation usually leads somewhere useful: a rise now, a plan for one later or honest feedback about what is standing in the way. Each of those is better than wondering."],
    ["Know your floor", "Start by checking the legal minimum for your role, because you might already be owed money.", "Before you ask for more, make sure you are getting at least what you are legally entitled to. If you are covered by an award or enterprise agreement, it sets minimum rates for your classification. Use the Fair Work Ombudsman's pay calculator or pay guides to check, and look closely at your classification level, since duties often grow faster than job titles. If your responsibilities now match a higher level, that is a strong, concrete point to raise. If you are paid below the minimum, that is not a pay rise conversation, it is an underpayment to be fixed, and the Fair Work Ombudsman can help. This lesson offers general information, not legal or financial advice. Once you know your floor, you can think about what you are actually worth in the market."],
    ["Know your market value", "Research what similar roles pay so your number is grounded in evidence, not hope.", "Your market value is roughly what another employer would pay someone with your skills, in your location, for similar work. Several job sites publish salary ranges, and many job ads now list pay, so spend an evening collecting examples for roles like yours. Industry surveys and professional associations can also help. In Australia, you are also legally free to talk about your own pay with colleagues if you choose to, and employers cannot stop you, which can give you useful real world information. Look for a range, not a single figure, and be honest about where you sit within it given your experience. Then decide on a specific amount to request, near the upper end of what you can justify, along with the lowest outcome you would be genuinely happy with."],
    ["Build your case", "Make your request about the value you add, not about your personal expenses.", "The strongest case focuses on what you contribute. Gather evidence: projects you delivered, problems you solved, money or time you saved, new responsibilities you took on, positive feedback from customers or colleagues. Write these down in a short document, one or two pages at most. Be specific: 'took over weekly rostering for a team of twelve' is better than 'helped out with admin'. Avoid leading with personal reasons such as rent or the cost of living. They are real pressures, but they are not reasons your employer should pay you more than someone else in the same role. If you have been given a bigger job without a bigger title or pay, say so plainly. Show how your role has changed since your pay was last set."],
    ["Pick the right moment", "Timing matters: ask when your value is visible and the budget is still open.", "Good moments include your annual performance review, soon after a clear win, when you take on new responsibilities, or a few months before budgets are set for the next financial year, which for many Australian organisations starts on 1 July. Bad moments include the middle of a crisis, straight after a round of redundancies or late on a Friday afternoon. Do not ambush your manager in a corridor. Instead, ask for a meeting: 'I would like to book half an hour to talk about my role and pay. Would next week work?' That gives them time to prepare and signals you are serious. If your workplace has a formal review cycle, find out when decisions are made, because by the time the review meeting happens the money may already be allocated."],
    ["Running the conversation", "State your request clearly, back it with evidence, then stop talking and listen.", "Open simply and warmly: say you enjoy the role and want to talk about your pay given how your work has grown. Then walk through two or three of your strongest examples. Make a specific request, such as an amount or a percentage, and briefly explain how you arrived at it. Then pause. Silence feels uncomfortable, but it gives your manager space to respond. Listen carefully to their answer and ask questions if anything is unclear. Stay calm and professional, even if the first response is disappointing. Avoid ultimatums unless you are truly prepared to leave, because a threat can damage trust even when it works. Close by agreeing on next steps and when you will hear back, then send a short email afterwards summarising what was discussed."],
    ["If the answer is no", "A no today can become a yes later if you turn it into a clear plan.", "Sometimes the budget genuinely is not there, or your manager does not yet see the case. Ask what would need to change for a rise to happen, and try to get specific, measurable goals and a date to revisit it, perhaps in three or six months. Put that agreement in writing. You can also ask about other things that matter to you: extra training, a study allowance, more flexible hours, a better title or additional leave. These can be easier to approve and still improve your working life. Then follow through, track your progress and bring it back at the agreed time. If promises are repeatedly broken, that is useful information too. Sometimes the best pay rise is a new job, and the case you prepared will help in those interviews."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Why check your award or agreement before asking for a rise?", ["Awards set maximum pay", "You might already be underpaid or at the wrong level", "It is required before any meeting", "Managers cannot read awards"], 1, "If you are below the legal minimum, that is an underpayment to fix, not a pay rise request."],
    [2, "In Australia, can an employer stop you discussing your own pay with colleagues?", ["Yes, always", "Only in small businesses", "Only with written notice", "No, employees can choose to share it"], 3, "Employees have a legal right to disclose their own pay if they choose."],
    [3, "Which is the strongest basis for a pay rise request?", ["Your rising rent", "That a friend earns more", "Evidence of the value you add and how your role has grown", "How long since your last holiday"], 2, "Requests built on contribution and responsibility are more persuasive than personal costs."],
    [4, "Why might asking well before a review meeting help?", ["Budgets may already be set by the time of the review", "Reviews are illegal", "Managers forget meetings", "It guarantees a yes"], 0, "Pay decisions are often made before the formal review, so early timing can matter."],
    [6, "What is a constructive response to a no?", ["Agree on specific goals and a date to revisit", "Issue an ultimatum", "Stop doing extra tasks", "Never raise it again"], 0, "Turning a no into a measurable plan gives you a clear path to a future yes."]
  ]);

WB.L("running-a-good-meeting", "life", "beginner", "Running a meeting worth attending", "Most of us have sat through a meeting that should have been an email. Here is how to run one people are glad they came to.",
  [
    ["The cost of a bad meeting", "A meeting uses everyone's time at once, so its cost is multiplied by the number of people in the room.", "Picture eight people in a one hour meeting. That is not one hour of work, it is eight hours, nearly a full working day, gone. If the meeting drifts, ends without a decision and needs a follow up, the cost doubles. Bad meetings also drain energy, interrupt focused work and teach people that their time does not matter. Yet good meetings are powerful. They can settle in thirty minutes a question that would take two weeks of emails, help a team feel connected and get everyone moving in the same direction. The difference is rarely luck. Good meetings are designed, and the person running them makes a handful of simple choices before, during and after. Anyone can learn them, whether you lead a team or are organising your first catch up."],
    ["Should this be a meeting?", "Before booking anything, check that a meeting is the best tool for the job.", "Start by asking what you want to achieve. If the goal is simply to share information that needs no discussion, an email, a short document or a recorded update is often better, because people can read it when it suits them. Meetings are best for things that need live conversation: making a decision, solving a tricky problem, building agreement on a plan, brainstorming or handling a sensitive issue. If you cannot write the purpose in one sentence, you are not ready to book the meeting. Try finishing this line: 'By the end of this meeting, we will have...'. For example, 'chosen a supplier for the new uniforms' or 'agreed who covers the December roster'. That sentence becomes your north star, and it goes right at the top of the invitation."],
    ["Invite the right people", "Fewer, better chosen attendees make for faster and more honest discussions.", "Every extra person adds opinions, side conversations and scheduling headaches. Invite only those who need to make the decision, have information you cannot get elsewhere or must carry out what is agreed. People who just need to know the outcome can get a summary afterwards. Think about who will be affected by the decision, too, because leaving out a key person often means redoing the meeting later. Be thoughtful about timing. Respect different working hours and flexible arrangements, and avoid booking meetings at times when part time staff or parents on school runs cannot attend. Choose a length that fits the task: many meetings that default to an hour could be done in twenty five or fifty minutes, leaving a buffer between back to back calls so people can stretch, refill and arrive ready."],
    ["Write a real agenda", "A useful agenda lists questions to answer, not just topics to talk about.", "An agenda that says 'budget, rosters, other business' tells nobody what to prepare. A better agenda turns each item into a question and gives it a time. For example: 'Which of the two venue quotes do we accept? Ten minutes. Read the attached comparison first.' Put the most important item first, while energy is high, and send the agenda with any reading at least a day ahead. Ask attendees to come having read it, and say so plainly. Some teams start with a few quiet minutes for everyone to read the key document together, which levels the playing field and means nobody bluffs. If someone wants to add an item, ask them to tell you before the meeting so you can plan the time. An agenda is a promise about how you will use people's time."],
    ["Run the room", "Start on time, keep the discussion on track and make sure every voice is heard.", "Begin on time, even if someone is late, and restate the purpose in a sentence. Your job as chair is to guide, not to dominate. Keep an eye on the clock and gently steer drifting conversations: 'That is a good point, let us park it and come back if we have time.' Keep a visible parking lot list for those side issues so people know they have not been ignored. Draw out quieter people by asking them directly, and politely rein in those who speak at length. In hybrid meetings, where some people are in the room and others are online, make sure remote attendees can hear, see shared material and get a fair turn, perhaps by calling on them first. If the discussion is going in circles, summarise where things stand and ask what is needed to decide."],
    ["End with decisions and actions", "Every meeting should end with clear decisions, named owners and due dates.", "The last five minutes are the most important, yet they are often squeezed out. Leave time to wrap up properly. Read back what was decided, in plain words, so everyone hears the same thing. Then list the actions: what will be done, who will do it and by when. An action without a name next to it usually does not happen. If something could not be decided, agree on what is needed to decide it and when. Check whether anyone disagrees with the summary before you close. Finishing a few minutes early is a gift people remember. A simple test of a good meeting is whether every attendee could tell a colleague afterwards, in one or two sentences, what was decided and what they personally need to do next."],
    ["Follow up quickly", "A short written summary sent the same day turns talk into action.", "Within a few hours, send a brief note to everyone who attended and anyone else who needs to know. Keep it short: the decisions made, the actions with owners and dates, and any open questions. Share it where your team can find it later, such as a shared folder or channel, so new people can catch up without another meeting. Then check in on the actions when they are due. Over time, review your regular meetings. Ask the team whether the weekly catch up still earns its place, and be willing to shorten it, change it or cancel it. Teams that regularly prune their meeting calendars often find they get more done. And when you attend someone else's meeting, you can quietly help by asking, 'What do we want to decide today?'"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is an eight person, one hour meeting costly?", ["It uses about eight hours of combined working time", "Meeting rooms are expensive", "It is only one hour", "Meetings are taxed"], 0, "Everyone's time is used at once, so the cost multiplies with each attendee."],
    [1, "When is a meeting usually better than an email?", ["To announce a public holiday", "To share a document nobody needs to discuss", "To make a decision that needs live discussion", "To send a reminder"], 2, "Meetings suit decisions, problem solving and sensitive issues that need conversation."],
    [3, "What makes an agenda item more useful?", ["Making it a single vague word", "Framing it as a question with a time and any pre reading", "Adding it at the last minute", "Putting the key item last"], 1, "Question based items with times and reading tell people exactly how to prepare."],
    [4, "What is a 'parking lot' in a meeting?", ["A list of off topic issues to return to later", "Where latecomers wait", "A break between meetings", "A list of people not invited"], 0, "Parking side issues keeps the meeting on track without dismissing them."],
    [5, "What should every action agreed in a meeting have?", ["A long explanation", "A vote by the whole company", "A slide deck", "A named owner and a due date"], 3, "Actions without an owner and a date usually do not happen."]
  ]);

WB.L("giving-and-receiving-feedback", "life", "intermediate", "Giving and receiving feedback at work", "Feedback can lift a team or wreck a week. A few practical techniques make it clearer, kinder and far more useful.",
  [
    ["Why feedback goes wrong", "Most feedback fails because it is vague, late or delivered in a way that triggers defence.", "Think of the last time someone said 'Can I give you some feedback?' Your heart probably sped up a little. Feedback feels personal because work is tied to our sense of competence and belonging. When it is delivered badly, people stop listening and start defending. Common problems are easy to spot. Feedback that is vague, such as 'you need to be more professional', gives nothing to act on. Feedback saved up for an annual review arrives months too late to be useful. Feedback mixed with frustration sounds like an attack. And praise that is generic, like 'great job', feels hollow. The good news is that both giving and receiving feedback are skills. A few simple structures can make conversations shorter, clearer and much less painful for everyone."],
    ["Use situation, behaviour, impact", "Describe when it happened, what the person did and the effect it had.", "One widely used structure, developed by the Center for Creative Leadership, is called situation, behaviour, impact. First, name the situation: 'In Tuesday's client meeting'. Second, describe the specific behaviour you saw or heard, without guessing at motives: 'you interrupted the client twice while they were explaining the problem'. Third, explain the impact: 'they seemed frustrated and we did not get the full picture'. This keeps the conversation anchored in facts rather than character judgements like 'you are rude'. Then pause and invite their view: 'How did it look from your side?' There may be context you missed. The same structure works brilliantly for praise: 'When you stayed calm with that upset customer this morning, the whole queue relaxed, and she thanked us on the way out.'"],
    ["Timing and setting", "Give feedback soon, in private for criticism, and when both of you can actually listen.", "Feedback is most useful close to the event, while memories are fresh, ideally within a day or two. But soon does not mean straight away in the heat of the moment. If you are angry, wait until you can speak calmly. Give constructive criticism in private, never in front of colleagues or customers. Praise can be public, though some people prefer it quietly, so learn what your team members like. Ask before diving in: 'Have you got ten minutes? I would like to talk about the handover yesterday.' That small courtesy gives the other person a moment to prepare. Avoid delivering important feedback by text or chat, where tone is easily misread; a conversation, in person or by video, usually works better, followed up in writing if needed."],
    ["Making it about the future", "Good feedback ends with what to do next, agreed together.", "Once you have described what happened and heard the other person's view, shift the conversation forward. Ask questions such as 'What could we try next time?' or 'What would help?' People are far more committed to solutions they helped shape. If you have a specific request, say it clearly: 'In client meetings, please let them finish before you jump in. Jot notes and raise points at the end.' Agree on one or two changes, not ten. Then follow up. Notice when things improve and say so, because recognising progress makes change stick. Some people find it helpful to think of feedback as coaching rather than judgement: the goal is not to win an argument about the past but to make the next week go better for both of you."],
    ["Receiving feedback well", "Listen, ask questions and thank the person, even when it stings.", "Being on the receiving end is often harder. When feedback lands, your instinct may be to explain, justify or counter attack. Try to resist that, at least at first. Breathe, listen to the whole message and ask clarifying questions: 'Can you give me an example?' or 'What would good look like?' Summarise what you heard to check you understood. Then thank them, even if you disagree, because giving feedback takes effort and some courage. You do not have to accept every point on the spot. It is fine to say, 'I want to think about that and come back to you.' Later, when you are calmer, sort what you heard into three piles: useful and true, partly true and simply not right. Act on the first pile."],
    ["Asking for feedback", "Asking for specific feedback regularly makes it easier to hear and much more useful.", "Waiting for feedback to arrive means you get it on someone else's schedule, often when something has gone wrong. Instead, ask for it. Make your request specific, because 'Any feedback?' usually produces 'No, all good'. Try 'What is one thing I could do to make my reports more useful for you?' or 'In that presentation, what part landed and what part lost people?' Ask a mix of people: your manager, peers and, if you lead a team, the people who report to you, who may need extra reassurance that honesty is safe. When someone gives you honest input, thank them and show that you acted on it. People who see their feedback used are much more likely to offer more, and over time the conversations get easier for everyone."],
    ["Feedback and leadership", "If you lead others, how you handle feedback sets the tone for the whole team.", "Leaders shape a team's feedback culture more than any policy does. If you get defensive when challenged, people learn to stay quiet, and you lose early warnings about problems. If you thank people for raising concerns, even uncomfortable ones, they learn it is safe to speak. Make feedback routine rather than rare, with regular one to one catch ups that include a few minutes on what is going well and what could improve. Keep a light record of praise and concerns through the year so reviews contain no surprises. Be careful to separate feedback on behaviour from formal performance or disciplinary processes, which have their own rules and fairness requirements. For guidance on handling performance issues in Australian workplaces, the Fair Work Ombudsman publishes free guides for managers and employees."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "In the situation, behaviour, impact model, what should the behaviour part describe?", ["The person's character", "Their likely motives", "What you specifically saw or heard", "How long they have worked there"], 2, "Describing observable behaviour keeps feedback factual and less personal."],
    [2, "Where is constructive criticism best given?", ["In private", "In a team meeting", "In front of customers", "In a group chat"], 0, "Criticism given privately is less likely to cause embarrassment and defensiveness."],
    [3, "Why involve the other person in deciding what to do next?", ["It shortens the meeting to one minute", "People commit more to solutions they helped shape", "It avoids saying anything specific", "It is required by law"], 1, "Shared solutions tend to stick better than instructions handed down."],
    [4, "What is a good first response when receiving tough feedback?", ["Explain why they are wrong", "Bring up their mistakes", "Leave the room", "Listen and ask clarifying questions"], 3, "Listening fully and asking for examples helps you understand before reacting."],
    [5, "Which request is most likely to produce useful feedback?", ["'Any feedback?'", "'What is one thing that would make my reports more useful?'", "'I did well, right?'", "'Let me know if there are problems.'"], 1, "Specific questions invite specific, actionable answers."]
  ]);

WB.L("burnout-and-disconnecting", "life", "intermediate", "Burnout and the right to disconnect", "Burnout is more than a bad week. Learn the warning signs, what you can change and what Australian workplace rules say about switching off.",
  [
    ["What burnout actually is", "Burnout is a work related pattern of exhaustion, cynicism and a sense of not getting anywhere.", "Jess used to love her job. Now Sunday nights bring a knot in her stomach, she snaps at colleagues and tasks that once took an hour drag on all afternoon. She is not lazy and she is not simply tired: she may be burning out. The World Health Organization describes burnout as an occupational phenomenon, not a medical condition, resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. It has three features: feelings of energy depletion or exhaustion, increased mental distance from your job or feelings of negativity or cynicism about it, and reduced professional effectiveness. Burnout is about work specifically, which matters, because the solutions often involve how work is organised, not just personal resilience. This lesson is general information, not medical, legal or professional advice."],
    ["Spotting the warning signs", "Burnout builds slowly, so learn to notice the early signals in yourself and others.", "Burnout rarely arrives overnight. Early signs can include trouble switching off in the evening, poor sleep, constant tiredness that rest does not fix, irritability, dreading work, getting sick more often and making uncharacteristic mistakes. You might notice you have stopped caring about things that used to matter, or that you feel numb about work. Others may notice before you do, so take it seriously if a partner or friend says you seem different. These signs can also overlap with other health issues, including depression and anxiety, so it is important not to self diagnose. If you are struggling, speak with your GP, who can check what is going on and connect you with support. Healthdirect and Beyond Blue also offer free, reliable information and phone support around the clock."],
    ["Why it happens", "Burnout is driven mostly by working conditions, not personal weakness.", "Researchers who study burnout often point to a mismatch between people and their work in several areas. These include workload that is too heavy for too long, a lack of control over how you do your job, too little recognition or reward, a breakdown of community such as conflict or isolation, a sense that things are unfair, and a clash between your values and what you are asked to do. Long hours alone do not always cause burnout; people can work hard on things they care about and feel energised. The danger zone is high demands combined with low control and little support. Understanding this helps in two ways. It reduces self blame, and it points you towards practical changes, some of which are within your control and some of which are your employer's responsibility."],
    ["Employers have duties too", "Australian work health and safety laws cover psychological health, not just physical safety.", "Under work health and safety laws, employers must, so far as is reasonably practicable, ensure the health and safety of workers, and health includes psychological health. In recent years, most Australian jurisdictions have introduced regulations that spell out how employers should manage psychosocial hazards, which are aspects of work that can cause psychological harm. Examples include excessive job demands, poor support, low role clarity, bullying, harassment and poor workplace relationships. This means a sustained, unmanageable workload is not just your personal problem; it is a workplace risk your employer should be identifying and controlling. Safe Work Australia and your state or territory regulator publish guidance on this. Many employers also offer an Employee Assistance Program, which typically provides free, confidential short term counselling, so it is worth asking whether yours does."],
    ["The right to disconnect", "Many Australian employees can now refuse unreasonable contact outside working hours.", "In 2024, Australia added a right to disconnect to the Fair Work Act. It began applying to employees of non small business employers in August 2024 and was extended to small business employees in August 2025. In broad terms, it means an employee can refuse to monitor, read or respond to contact, or attempted contact, from their employer or a third party such as a client outside their working hours, unless that refusal is unreasonable. Whether a refusal is unreasonable depends on factors such as the reason for the contact, how it is made, whether you are paid to be available, your role and your personal circumstances. Emergencies and genuine on call arrangements are treated differently. For details on how this applies to you, check the Fair Work Ombudsman website."],
    ["Practical boundaries that work", "Small, consistent habits help protect your time and energy.", "Rules help, but daily habits matter just as much. Set a clear end to your workday, and mark it with a small ritual such as closing the laptop, writing tomorrow's top three tasks or going for a walk. Turn off work notifications on your phone outside hours, or use focus modes that only let urgent contacts through. If you manage others, avoid sending late night emails, or schedule them to arrive in the morning, because a message from the boss at ten pm can feel like an instruction. Protect your leave and actually take it, including switching off fully while away. During the day, take your breaks away from the desk. Keep up sleep, movement and time with people you enjoy. None of these fix a broken workload, but they help you recover."],
    ["Talking to your manager", "Raise workload early, with specifics and options, rather than waiting until you break.", "Many people wait until they are close to collapse before saying anything. It is far easier to fix things earlier. Book a private conversation with your manager and describe the situation concretely: what is on your plate, how many hours it is taking and what is slipping. Avoid framing it as a confession of weakness. Instead, frame it as a resourcing problem you want to solve together. Bring options: which tasks could be paused, delegated, delayed or done more simply. Ask your manager to help you prioritise: 'If I can only do three of these well this month, which three matter most?' Follow up the conversation in writing. If nothing changes, consider talking to HR, a union, your health and safety representative or the Fair Work Ombudsman for information about your options."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does the World Health Organization describe burnout?", ["As a personality flaw", "As an occupational phenomenon, not a medical condition", "As a type of virus", "As a legal status"], 1, "The WHO classes burnout as an occupational phenomenon resulting from unmanaged chronic workplace stress."],
    [2, "Which combination is most strongly linked to burnout?", ["Low demands and high control", "Interesting work and good support", "High demands, low control and little support", "Short weeks and regular praise"], 2, "Heavy demands with little control or support form the classic danger zone."],
    [3, "What does Australian work health and safety law say about psychological health?", ["Employers must manage psychosocial risks as far as reasonably practicable", "Only physical injuries count", "It is entirely the worker's responsibility", "It applies only to the public service"], 0, "WHS duties cover psychological as well as physical health."],
    [4, "Under the right to disconnect, when can an employee's refusal of after hours contact be unacceptable?", ["Never", "Whenever the boss is busy", "Only on weekends", "When the refusal is unreasonable in the circumstances"], 3, "The right applies unless the refusal is unreasonable, judged on factors such as role, pay and the reason for contact."],
    [6, "What is a constructive way to raise an unmanageable workload?", ["Say nothing and work harder", "Describe it specifically and bring options to prioritise", "Quietly stop doing tasks", "Complain to customers"], 1, "Specific details and options make it easier for a manager to help fix the problem."]
  ]);

WB.L("contractor-or-employee", "life", "advanced", "Working on an ABN: contractor or employee?", "More Australians are picking up work as contractors. Here is how to tell if it is genuine, and what to sort out if you go it alone.",
  [
    ["A growing way to work", "Contracting can offer freedom, but it shifts many responsibilities from the business to you.", "Liam is offered a job building websites: good money, but they ask him to get an ABN and send invoices instead of going on the payroll. It sounds simple. Yet the difference between being an employee and an independent contractor changes almost everything: who pays your tax, who pays your super, whether you get paid leave, whether you are covered by an award and what happens if the work dries up. Plenty of people contract happily, from tradies and consultants to designers and side hustlers, and enjoy the flexibility. But the label is not just paperwork. This lesson explains how the distinction works and what to set up if you are genuinely contracting. It is general information, not legal, tax or financial advice, so check your own situation with official sources."],
    ["What makes someone an employee", "The real nature of the working relationship matters more than what the contract calls it.", "There is no single test. Courts and tribunals look at the whole working relationship, including how it works in practice, not just what the paperwork says. Following changes to the Fair Work Act in 2024, the law makes clear that the real substance of the arrangement counts. Factors that tend to suggest you are an employee include the business controlling how, when and where you work, being paid for your time rather than a result, using the business's equipment, working only for them, being part of their team and being unable to send someone else to do the work. Factors suggesting genuine contracting include running your own business, choosing how to do the job, taking on financial risk, using your own tools, working for several clients and being able to delegate."],
    ["Sham contracting", "Calling an employee a contractor to avoid obligations is illegal.", "Sham contracting is when a business falsely presents what is really employment as an independent contracting arrangement. This can happen deliberately, to avoid paying super, leave and award rates, or through genuine misunderstanding. It is against the law for an employer to misrepresent employment as contracting, to dismiss an employee in order to re engage them as a contractor doing much the same work, or to pressure someone into becoming a contractor with a false statement. If you are really an employee, you may be entitled to back pay, super and leave even if you signed a contractor agreement and held an ABN. If you are unsure, the Fair Work Ombudsman has information on the difference, and the ATO offers an employee or contractor decision tool on its website."],
    ["Getting an ABN", "An Australian Business Number is free to apply for, but you must actually be running a business.", "If you are genuinely carrying on a business, you can apply for an Australian Business Number through the Australian Business Register at abr.gov.au. It is free, and you do not need to pay anyone to do it for you, though many websites offer to for a fee. You will need your tax file number and some details about your business activity. Not everyone is entitled to an ABN: you need to be carrying on an enterprise, not simply working as an employee or pursuing a hobby. Once you have one, quote it on your invoices, because businesses may be required to withhold tax from payments to suppliers who do not provide one. Business.gov.au is a good starting point for registrations, business names and choosing a structure such as sole trader."],
    ["Tax, GST and setting money aside", "As a contractor, no one withholds tax for you, so you need your own system.", "When you are paid as a contractor, usually the full invoice amount lands in your account with no tax taken out. That money is not all yours. You will need to include it in your tax return, and if your business income grows you may be placed into the ATO's pay as you go instalments system, which collects tax in advance through the year. Many contractors open a separate savings account and move a set share of every payment into it for tax. You must register for GST once your turnover reaches the threshold, and some industries, such as ride sourcing, must register regardless. Check current thresholds and rules on the ATO website. Keep good records of income and business expenses, and consider getting a registered tax agent to help."],
    ["Super, leave and insurance", "Contractors usually have to build their own safety net.", "Employees get paid leave, employer super and workers compensation cover. Genuine contractors often get none of these automatically, so your rates should cover them. Many contractors make voluntary super contributions themselves; check the ATO website and your fund for rules and caps. Note that under super law, some contractors who are paid mainly for their labour can still be entitled to super guarantee payments from the business that engages them, even with an ABN, so it is worth checking the ATO's guidance. Think about insurance as well: public liability and professional indemnity cover may be required by clients, and income protection can replace earnings if illness or injury stops you working. Without paid sick days or holidays, build time off into your plans, or you may find you have accidentally created a job with no breaks."],
    ["Pricing and protecting yourself", "Set rates that cover your real costs, and put agreements in writing.", "A common mistake is setting your rate by dividing an employee salary by working hours. As a contractor, you also need to cover super, leave you will not be paid for, insurance, equipment, software, accounting fees and quiet periods between jobs. That is why contract rates are often noticeably higher than the equivalent wage. Before starting, agree in writing on the scope of work, the price, payment terms, who owns the work you produce and how either side can end the arrangement. Send clear invoices with your ABN and payment details, and follow up late payments promptly. Some contractors and small businesses have protections against unfair contract terms under Australian law, and business.gov.au explains these. If a deal feels unclear or lopsided, getting independent legal advice before you sign can save trouble."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What matters most in deciding whether someone is an employee?", ["Whether they have an ABN", "What the contract is titled", "The real substance of the working relationship", "Whether they send invoices"], 2, "Courts look at the whole relationship and how it works in practice, not just the label."],
    [2, "What is sham contracting?", ["Hiring a genuine independent tradie", "Falsely presenting employment as a contracting arrangement", "Working for several clients", "Charging GST"], 1, "Misrepresenting employment as contracting to avoid obligations is illegal."],
    [3, "How much does it cost to apply for an ABN through the Australian Business Register?", ["Nothing, it is free", "A yearly fee set by each state", "One week's pay", "It depends on your bank"], 0, "Applying through abr.gov.au is free."],
    [4, "Why do many contractors move part of every payment into a separate account?", ["Banks require it", "To avoid needing an ABN", "To cover clients' super", "Because no tax has been withheld and it will be due later"], 3, "Contractors are usually paid in full and must cover their own tax."],
    [6, "Why are contract rates often higher than the equivalent hourly wage?", ["Contractors are always more skilled", "They must cover super, unpaid leave, insurance and quiet periods", "The law sets them higher", "Clients pay tax for them"], 1, "Rates need to cover costs and entitlements an employer would otherwise provide."]
  ]);

WB.L("leaving-a-job-well", "life", "beginner", "Leaving a job the right way", "How you leave a job can shape your references, your final pay and your reputation for years. Here is how to resign well.",
  [
    ["Endings are remembered", "People often remember how you left more clearly than how you worked.", "Industries are smaller than they look. The manager you leave behind may one day interview you, become your client or be called as your referee. The colleague who covered your workload after a messy exit may end up at your next workplace. That is why how you leave matters. A good exit protects your references, makes sure you receive everything you are owed and leaves the door open for future opportunities. A bad exit, such as walking out mid shift, posting complaints online or vanishing without a handover, can follow you for years. Leaving well does not mean pretending everything was perfect. It means being clear, professional and fair, even if you are glad to be going. This lesson is general information, not legal advice; the Fair Work Ombudsman has details for your situation."],
    ["Check your notice period", "Your award, agreement or contract usually says how much notice you must give.", "Before you resign, find out how much notice you need to give. For many employees, this is set by their award, enterprise agreement or employment contract, so check all three. Notice periods of one to four weeks are common, often depending on how long you have worked there, while senior roles may require longer. Casual employees often have different rules, and in many cases need not give notice, but check your award. If you do not give the required notice, some awards and agreements allow your employer to deduct an amount from your final pay, so it is worth getting this right. Your employer may also choose to pay you instead of having you work out the notice period. Read the clauses carefully, and ask HR if anything is unclear."],
    ["Have the conversation first", "Tell your manager in person or by video, then confirm it in writing.", "Your manager should hear the news from you, not from the grapevine or an email that lands without warning. Ask for a short private meeting. Keep it simple and positive: say you have decided to leave, give your planned last day and thank them for the opportunity. You do not have to explain where you are going or why, and you should avoid using the meeting to air every grievance. If there are real problems worth raising, an exit interview or a separate conversation may be better. Then follow up with a short resignation letter or email. Include the date, your role, a clear statement that you are resigning, your intended last day and a brief thank you. Keep a copy for your records, since the date matters for your notice period."],
    ["Counter offers and pressure", "Think carefully before accepting a counter offer, and stay calm if the reaction is tough.", "Sometimes an employer responds to a resignation with a counter offer: more money, a new title or a promise that things will change. It can be flattering and occasionally it is exactly what you wanted. But pause and ask yourself why you started looking in the first place. If the reasons were about pay alone, a counter offer might solve the problem. If they were about culture, management, workload or growth, money rarely fixes those for long. Also consider why it took a resignation to get the offer. On the other side, some managers react badly, with anger or guilt. Stay polite, firm and brief. Your decision is yours to make. You must not be threatened or punished for exercising workplace rights, and the Fair Work Ombudsman can give information if things go wrong."],
    ["Hand over properly", "A clear handover is the best way to protect your reputation on the way out.", "Use your notice period to make life easy for whoever takes over your work. Write a handover document covering current projects, where key files and passwords are stored through approved channels, upcoming deadlines, regular tasks and important contacts. Note anything that is half finished and what needs to happen next. Offer to train a colleague or your replacement if there is time. Finish what you reasonably can and be honest about what you cannot. Keep turning up and working properly until your last day; coasting is noticed. Return equipment such as laptops, phones, keys and uniforms, and do not take confidential information, client lists or company files with you, which could breach your contract or the law. Say goodbye to colleagues and swap personal contact details with those you want to stay in touch with."],
    ["Final pay and entitlements", "Make sure your final pay includes everything you are owed, and keep your records.", "When you leave, your final pay should include your wages up to your last day and any other outstanding amounts. Under the National Employment Standards, unused annual leave is paid out when employment ends, including any leave loading if your award, agreement or contract provides for it. Unused personal or carer's leave is generally not paid out. Long service leave rules depend mostly on your state or territory and sometimes your award, so check whether you have an entitlement. Check your final payslip carefully against your last ordinary payslip and your leave balance. Make sure your final super contribution is paid, and download copies of your payslips before your access to work systems ends. If something is missing, raise it in writing, and contact the Fair Work Ombudsman if it is not resolved."],
    ["Leave the door open", "End on good terms, ask for references and keep your network warm.", "Before your last day, ask your manager or a senior colleague whether they would be willing to act as a referee, and confirm their preferred contact details. If you have an exit interview, give honest but constructive feedback; focus on what would help the organisation improve rather than personal attacks. Update your LinkedIn profile after you have finished, not before you have told your manager. Send a short farewell message to colleagues thanking them and sharing how they can reach you. Avoid criticising your old employer publicly, in interviews or online, even if they deserve it. Many people end up returning to a former employer years later, or working with old colleagues in new places. A graceful exit costs you very little and keeps those possibilities alive."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Where should you look to find your notice period?", ["Your award, agreement or contract", "The ATO website", "A social media post", "Your super fund statement"], 0, "Notice periods are usually set by your award, enterprise agreement or employment contract."],
    [2, "What is the best way to tell your manager you are leaving?", ["Ask a colleague to pass it on", "Post about it online first", "Leave a note on their desk", "Talk to them privately, then confirm in writing"], 3, "A private conversation followed by a written resignation is clear and respectful."],
    [3, "When might a counter offer fail to solve the problem?", ["When you left only because of pay", "When the real issues are culture, management or growth", "When it includes more money", "When it is in writing"], 1, "Money rarely fixes non financial reasons for leaving for long."],
    [5, "Under the National Employment Standards, which leave is paid out when you leave?", ["Personal or carer's leave", "Compassionate leave", "Unused annual leave", "Public holidays not yet taken"], 2, "Unused annual leave is paid out on termination, while personal leave generally is not."],
    [6, "When should you update your LinkedIn to show you are leaving?", ["Before telling your manager", "After you have told your manager and finished up", "Never", "The day you start looking"], 1, "Your manager should hear first, so update your profile afterwards."]
  ]);
